<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ title }}</title>
<style>
  :root {
    --bg: #ffffff; --fg: #1a1d21; --muted: #5c6470; --line: #dfe3e8;
    --panel: #f7f8fa; --accent: #2b5c8a; --warn: #8a5a00; --warn-bg: #fff6e0;
    --bad: #9b2226; --bad-bg: #fdeced; --ok: #1f6f43; --ok-bg: #e8f5ee;
  }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root {
      --bg: #14171a; --fg: #e6e9ed; --muted: #9aa4b0; --line: #2b3138;
      --panel: #1c2025; --accent: #7fb2e0; --warn: #e5b567; --warn-bg: #33290f;
      --bad: #e88a8d; --bad-bg: #3a1d1f; --ok: #7dcfa0; --ok-bg: #16301f;
    }
  }
  * { box-sizing: border-box; }
  body {
    margin: 0; padding: 2rem 1.25rem 4rem; background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg);
    font: 15px/1.6 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif;
  }
  .wrap { max-width: 62rem; margin: 0 auto; }
  h1 { font-size: 1.7rem; margin: 0 0 .25rem; letter-spacing: -0.01em; }
  h2 {
    font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 2.75rem 0 .75rem; padding-bottom: .4rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);
  }
  h3 { font-size: .95rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 .5rem; color: var(--muted);
       text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .05em; }
  .sub { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 2rem; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: .5rem 0 1rem; font-size: .875rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: .45rem .6rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  th { font-weight: 600; color: var(--muted); font-size: .8rem;
       text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .04em; }
  td.num, th.num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  .panel { background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line);
           border-radius: 8px; padding: 1rem 1.15rem; margin: 1rem 0; }
  .kv { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(11rem, auto) 1fr; gap: .3rem 1.25rem; }
  .kv dt { color: var(--muted); font-size: .85rem; }
  .kv dd { margin: 0; font-family: ui-monospace, "SF Mono", Menlo, monospace; font-size: .82rem;
           word-break: break-all; }
  .badge { display: inline-block; padding: .1rem .5rem; border-radius: 999px;
           font-size: .75rem; font-weight: 600; }
  .badge.ok { background: var(--ok-bg); color: var(--ok); }
  .badge.warn { background: var(--warn-bg); color: var(--warn); }
  .badge.bad { background: var(--bad-bg); color: var(--bad); }
  .callout { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: .6rem .9rem;
             background: var(--panel); margin: .75rem 0; font-size: .9rem; }
  .callout.warn { border-left-color: var(--warn); background: var(--warn-bg); }
  .callout.bad { border-left-color: var(--bad); background: var(--bad-bg); }
  figure.chart { margin: .75rem 0 1.25rem; }
  figure.chart .js-plotly-plot { width: 100%; }
  .funnel-bar { height: 1.35rem; background: var(--accent); border-radius: 3px; min-width: 2px; }
  pre { background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 6px;
        padding: .9rem; overflow-x: auto; font-size: .78rem; line-height: 1.45; }
  ul.tight { margin: .4rem 0; padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  ul.tight li { margin: .25rem 0; }
  .lim { margin: .9rem 0; }
  .lim strong { display: block; margin-bottom: .15rem; }
  .lim p { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: .9rem; }
  footer { margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 1rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
           color: var(--muted); font-size: .82rem; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="wrap">

  <h1>{{ title }}</h1>
  <p class="sub">
    morphline {{ provenance.pipeline_version }} &middot;
    {{ provenance.dataset }} ({{ provenance.dataset_version }}) &middot;
    {{ provenance.run_timestamp }}
  </p>

  {# ---- 1. Provenance (§2.8): a reader holding only this file must be able
         to reconstruct the run. ---- #}
  <h2>1. Provenance</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <dl class="kv">
      <dt>pipeline_version</dt><dd>{{ provenance.pipeline_version }}</dd>
      <dt>git_sha</dt>
      <dd>
        {{ provenance.git_sha or "unavailable" }}
        {% if provenance.git_dirty %}<span class="badge bad">dirty</span>{% endif %}
      </dd>
      <dt>container_image</dt><dd>{{ provenance.container_image or "not containerised" }}</dd>
      <dt>container_digest</dt><dd>{{ provenance.container_digest or "n/a" }}</dd>
      <dt>python_version</dt><dd>{{ provenance.python_version }}</dd>
      <dt>platform</dt><dd>{{ provenance.platform }}</dd>
      <dt>nextflow_version</dt><dd>{{ provenance.nextflow_version or "not run under Nextflow" }}</dd>
      <dt>parser_version</dt><dd>{{ provenance.parser_version }}</dd>
      <dt>freesurfer_version(s)</dt>
      <dd>{{ provenance.freesurfer_versions | join(", ") or "none observed" }}</dd>
      <dt>dataset</dt><dd>{{ provenance.dataset }} / {{ provenance.dataset_version }}</dd>
      <dt>input_path</dt><dd>{{ provenance.input_path or "generated fixtures" }}</dd>
      <dt>random_seed(s)</dt>
      <dd>
        {% for k, v in provenance.random_seeds.items() %}{{ k }}={{ v }} {% endfor %}
        {% if not provenance.random_seeds %}none{% endif %}
      </dd>
      <dt>duration_seconds</dt>
      <dd>{{ "%.2f"|format(provenance.duration_seconds) if provenance.duration_seconds else "n/a" }}</dd>
    </dl>
    <h3>Fully resolved configuration</h3>
    <pre>{{ resolved_config }}</pre>
  </div>

  {# ---- 2. Data accounting funnel (§1.6): every drop needs a stated cause. ---- #}
  <h2>2. Data accounting funnel</h2>

  {% if reconciliation_errors %}
  <div class="callout bad">
    <strong>Funnel does not reconcile.</strong> Unexplained loss is a bug, not a
    rounding error.
    <ul class="tight">
      {% for err in reconciliation_errors %}<li>{{ err }}</li>{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </div>
  {% else %}
  <div class="callout">
    <span class="badge ok">reconciles exactly</span>
    Every drop below has a stated cause.
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {% set max_count = funnel | map(attribute='count') | max %}
  <div class="scroll">
{% if charts.get("funnel") %}
  <figure class="chart">{{ charts["funnel"] | safe }}</figure>
{% endif %}
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Boundary</th><th>Unit</th><th class="num">Count</th>
        <th class="num">Lost</th><th>Causes</th><th style="width:22%">&nbsp;</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in funnel %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.boundary }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.unit }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ row.count }}</td>
        <td class="num">
          {% if row.lost %}<span class="badge warn">{{ row.lost }}</span>{% else %}0{% endif %}
        </td>
        <td>{{ row.causes or "—" }}</td>
        <td>
          <div class="funnel-bar"
               style="width: {{ (100 * row.count / max_count) if max_count else 0 }}%"></div>
        </td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  {% if parse_failures %}
  <h3>Parse failures by reason code</h3>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Reason code</th><th class="num">Files</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for code, n in parse_failures.items() %}
      <tr><td>{{ code }}</td><td class="num">{{ n }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

  {% if missingness %}
  <h3>Missingness by cause</h3>
{% if charts.get("missingness") %}
  <figure class="chart">{{ charts["missingness"] | safe }}</figure>
{% endif %}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th class="num">Sessions</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for cause, n in missingness.items() %}
      <tr><td>{{ cause }}</td><td class="num">{{ n }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

  <h3>Completers vs non-completers</h3>
{% if charts.get("completer_smd") %}
  <figure class="chart">{{ charts["completer_smd"] | safe }}</figure>
{% endif %}
  {% if not completers.get("applicable") %}
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Not applicable.</strong> {{ completers.get("reason", "no comparison was run") }}.
  </div>
  {% else %}
  <p class="sub">
    {{ completers.definition | capitalize }}. The MAR assumption behind the
    longitudinal estimates is weakest where dropout is caused by the process
    being studied, so baseline differences between these groups are reported
    rather than assumed away (§2.5.4). Standardized mean differences are given
    instead of p-values: the question is whether the groups differ enough to
    matter, not whether the sample is large enough to detect that they do.
  </p>

  <dl class="kv">
    <dt>completers</dt><dd>{{ completers.n_completers }} subjects</dd>
    <dt>non-completers</dt><dd>{{ completers.n_non_completers }} subjects</dd>
    <dt>no baseline data</dt>
    <dd>{{ completers.n_without_baseline }} subjects, not comparable</dd>
  </dl>

  {% if completers.continuous %}
  <div class="scroll">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Baseline covariate</th>
        <th class="num">Completers</th><th class="num">Non-completers</th>
        <th class="num">SMD</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for name, stats in completers.continuous.items() %}
      <tr>
        <td><code>{{ name }}</code></td>
        <td class="num">
          {% if stats.completers.mean is not none %}
          {{ "%.1f"|format(stats.completers.mean) }} &plusmn; {{ "%.1f"|format(stats.completers.sd) }}
          (n={{ stats.completers.n }})
          {% else %}—{% endif %}
        </td>
        <td class="num">
          {% if stats.non_completers.mean is not none %}
          {{ "%.1f"|format(stats.non_completers.mean) }} &plusmn; {{ "%.1f"|format(stats.non_completers.sd) }}
          (n={{ stats.non_completers.n }})
          {% else %}—{% endif %}
        </td>
        <td class="num">
          {% if stats.standardized_difference is not none %}
          {% if stats.standardized_difference | abs >= 0.1 %}
          <span class="badge bad">{{ "%.2f"|format(stats.standardized_difference) }}</span>
          {% else %}{{ "%.2f"|format(stats.standardized_difference) }}{% endif %}
          {% else %}—{% endif %}
        </td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {% if completers.categorical %}
  <div class="scroll">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Baseline covariate</th><th>Completers</th><th>Non-completers</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for name, stats in completers.categorical.items() %}
      <tr>
        <td><code>{{ name }}</code></td>
        <td>
          {% for level, share in stats.completers.items() %}
          {{ level }} {{ "%.0f%%"|format(100 * share) }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}
          {% else %}—{% endfor %}
        </td>
        <td>
          {% for level, share in stats.non_completers.items() %}
          {{ level }} {{ "%.0f%%"|format(100 * share) }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}
          {% else %}—{% endfor %}
        </td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  {% endif %}
  {% endif %}

  {% if completers.get("notes") %}
  <div class="callout">
    <ul class="tight">
      {% for note in completers.notes %}<li>{{ note }}</li>{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  <h3>Metadata coverage</h3>
  <div class="scroll">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Distribution</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for field, counts in metadata_coverage.items() %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ field }}</td>
        <td>
          {% for k, v in counts.items() %}{{ k }}: {{ v }}{% if not loop.last %} &middot; {% endif %}{% endfor %}
          {% if not counts %}—{% endif %}
        </td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  {% if batch_sizes %}
  <h3>Batch sizes</h3>
{% if charts.get("batch_sizes") %}
  <figure class="chart">{{ charts["batch_sizes"] | safe }}</figure>
{% endif %}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Site</th><th class="num">Subjects</th><th class="num">Observations</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for site, n in batch_sizes.get('subjects_per_site', {}).items() %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ site }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ n }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ batch_sizes.get('observations_per_site', {}).get(site, 0) }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

  {% if accounting_notes %}
  <div class="callout warn">
    <ul class="tight">
      {% for note in accounting_notes %}<li>{{ note }}</li>{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {# ---- 3. QC (§2.4) ---- #}
  <h2>3. Quality control</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    QC <strong>classifies</strong>; the analysis layer <strong>decides</strong>.
    <code>qc_status</code> is three-level and separate from
    <code>analysis_included</code>, which comes from an explicit configured
    policy — so review-worthy observations stay visible instead of vanishing
    into an exclusion list. No check here can distinguish segmentation failure
    from biology, and none of them claims to.
  </div>
{% if charts.get("qc_status") %}
  <figure class="chart">{{ charts["qc_status"] | safe }}</figure>
{% endif %}
{% if charts.get("qc_flags") %}
  <figure class="chart">{{ charts["qc_flags"] | safe }}</figure>
{% endif %}
  {% if qc_summary and qc_summary.by_status %}
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th class="num">Observations</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for status, n in qc_summary.by_status.items() %}
      <tr><td>{{ status }}</td><td class="num">{{ n }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

  {# ---- 4. Harmonization (§2.3) ---- #}
  <h2>4. Harmonization diagnostics</h2>

  {% if confound %}
  <div class="callout {{ 'bad' if not confound.interpretable else '' }}">
    <strong>Scanner &times; time confounding: {{ confound.severity }}.</strong>
    {{ confound.message }}
  </div>
  <dl class="kv">
    <dt>site explains var(time)</dt><dd>{{ "%.3f"|format(confound.max_site_time_r2) }}</dd>
    <dt>site/time correlation</dt><dd>{{ "%.3f"|format(confound.correlation) }}</dd>
    <dt>longitudinal estimates</dt>
    <dd>
      {% if confound.interpretable %}
        <span class="badge ok">interpretable with stated caveats</span>
      {% else %}
        <span class="badge bad">NOT interpretable as biology</span>
      {% endif %}
    </dd>
  </dl>
  {% endif %}

  {% if confound_crosstab %}
  <h3>Site &times; time-from-baseline crosstab</h3>
{% if charts.get("confound") %}
  <figure class="chart">{{ charts["confound"] | safe }}</figure>
{% endif %}
  <div class="scroll">{{ confound_crosstab | safe }}</div>
  {% endif %}

  {% if small_batches %}
  <h3>Batches below min_batch_size</h3>
  <p class="sub">
    Reported, never silently dropped. The threshold is a conservative
    engineering default for stability, not a methodological law — adequacy
    depends on the number of batches, covariate balance within batch, outcome
    variance, and the magnitude of the batch effect relative to it.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr>
      <th>Batch</th><th class="num">Subjects</th><th class="num">Observations</th>
      <th>Sex</th><th>Diagnosis</th><th class="num">Age (mean)</th>
    </tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for batch, n in small_batches.items() %}
      {% set comp = small_batch_composition.get(batch, {}) %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ batch }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ n }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ comp.get("n_observations", "&mdash;") }}</td>
        <td>{% for k, v in (comp.get("sex") or {}).items() %}{{ k }}&nbsp;{{ v }} {% endfor %}</td>
        <td>{% for k, v in (comp.get("dx_baseline") or {}).items() %}{{ k }}&nbsp;{{ v }} {% endfor %}</td>
        <td class="num">
          {% if comp.get("age_baseline_mean") is not none %}
          {{ "%.1f"|format(comp.age_baseline_mean) }}
          {% else %}&mdash;{% endif %}
        </td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

  {% if combat %}
  <h3>Estimated batch parameters</h3>
  <p class="sub">
    ComBat (Johnson et al. 2007), parametric empirical Bayes, implemented
    in-repo. &gamma; is the additive batch term shown in the measure's native
    units; &delta; is the residual scale, where 1.0 means the batch's spread
    matches the pooled spread. &gamma; is identified only up to a size-weighted
    centering, so the terms sum to zero across batches by construction and an
    individual value is meaningful only relative to the others.
    {% if harmonization_estimation.get("estimation_set") == "analysis_included" %}
    Terms were estimated on QC-passing observations only
    ({{ harmonization_estimation.n_estimation_rows }} of
    {{ harmonization_estimation.n_rows }} rows) and applied to every row.
    {% endif %}
  </p>
  {% if combat.covariates_dropped %}
  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Covariates left out of the design matrix:</strong>
    {% for name, reason in combat.covariates_dropped.items() %}
    <code>{{ name }}</code> ({{ reason }}){{ ", " if not loop.last }}
    {% endfor %}.
    Their variation is not protected from being absorbed into the batch terms.
  </div>
  {% endif %}
  <div class="scroll">
  <table>
    <thead><tr>
      <th>Region</th><th>Measure</th><th>Batch</th>
      <th class="num">n</th><th class="num">&gamma; (native)</th><th class="num">&delta;</th>
    </tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for p in combat.parameters %}
      {% for batch, gamma in p.gamma_native.items() %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ p.region if loop.first }}</td>
        <td>{{ p.measure_type if loop.first }}</td>
        <td>{{ batch }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ p.n_per_batch[batch] }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.3g"|format(gamma) }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.3f"|format(p.delta_star[batch]) }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {% if combat and combat.skipped %}
  <h3>Regions left unharmonized</h3>
  <p class="sub">
    Reason-coded rather than dropped. These observations keep their original
    values and remain in the output.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for key, reason in combat.skipped.items() %}
      <tr><td>{{ key }}</td><td><code>{{ reason }}</code></td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

  {% if harmonization_pooling %}
  <h3>Pooled batches</h3>
  <p class="sub">
    Pooling asserts the merged batches are exchangeable. That claim is not
    checkable from the data, and it changed the estimate, so it is recorded
    here and in the provenance block.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr>
      <th>Pooled label</th><th>Members</th><th>Scanner</th><th class="num">Subjects</th>
    </tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for m in harmonization_pooling.get("merged", []) %}
      <tr>
        <td><code>{{ m.label }}</code></td>
        <td>{{ m.members | join(", ") }}</td>
        <td>{{ m.scanner_manufacturer }} {{ m.scanner_model }} @ {{ m.field_strength_tesla }}T</td>
        <td class="num">{{ m.n_subjects }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% endif %}

  {% if harmonization_notes %}
  <div class="callout">
    <ul class="tight">
      {% for note in harmonization_notes %}<li>{{ note }}</li>{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {# ---- 5. Model results (§2.5) ---- #}
  <h2>5. Model results</h2>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Sensitivity analysis, not validated biological inference.</strong>
    Harmonized and unharmonized estimates show how conclusions depend on the
    harmonization assumption; they do not establish which is correct.
  </div>

  <dl class="kv">
    <dt>specification</dt>
    <dd>value ~ age_baseline + time + dx_baseline + time:dx_baseline + sex + etiv_baseline</dd>
    <dt>random effects</dt><dd>intercept + slope on time, grouped by subject_id</dd>
    <dt>primary family</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>{{ primary_term }}</code> across {{ model.family_size }} regional
      tests (14 structures &times; 2 hemispheres); {{ family_sizes.get(primary_term, 0) }}
      carried a p-value and were corrected
    </dd>
    <dt>FDR alpha</dt><dd>{{ model.fdr_alpha }}, applied within each family separately</dd>
    <dt>convergence rate</dt><dd>{{ "%.0f%%"|format(100 * model.convergence_rate) }}</dd>
    <dt>modeled observations</dt><dd>{{ model.n_modeled_observations }}</dd>
  </dl>
{% if charts.get("forest") %}
  <figure class="chart">{{ charts["forest"] | safe }}</figure>
{% endif %}

  <div class="scroll">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Region</th><th>Measure</th><th class="num">N obs</th><th class="num">N subj</th>
        <th>Converged</th><th class="num">Estimate</th><th class="num">SE</th>
        <th class="num">p</th><th class="num">q</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in model_rows %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.region }}</td>
        <td>{{ row.measure_type }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ row.n_observations }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ row.n_subjects }}</td>
        <td>
          {% if row.converged %}<span class="badge ok">yes</span>
          {% else %}<span class="badge bad">no</span>{% endif %}
        </td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4f"|format(row.estimate) if row.estimate is not none else "—" }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4f"|format(row.std_error) if row.std_error is not none else "—" }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4g"|format(row.p_value) if row.p_value is not none else "—" }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4g"|format(row.q_value) if row.q_value is not none else "—" }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p class="sub">
    Estimate and p-value are for the <code>time &times; diagnosis</code>
    interaction — the primary hypothesis. Hemispheres are independent tests and
    are counted in the multiplicity family.
  </p>

  <h3>Secondary families — exploratory</h3>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Each secondary effect is its own family, corrected separately.</strong>
    BH-FDR is applied within each column below independently, never across
    them and never together with the primary family. A <em>q</em> here is not
    comparable to a <em>q</em> in the primary table, and none of these tests
    are part of the declared hypothesis (§2.5.3).
  </div>

  <div class="scroll">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th rowspan="2">Region</th>
        {% for term in secondary_terms %}
        <th colspan="3"><code>{{ term }}</code><br>
          <span class="sub">{{ family_sizes.get(term, 0) }} tests</span></th>
        {% endfor %}
      </tr>
      <tr>
        {% for term in secondary_terms %}
        <th class="num">Estimate</th><th class="num">p</th><th class="num">q</th>
        {% endfor %}
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in secondary_rows %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.region }}</td>
        {% for t in row.terms %}
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4f"|format(t.estimate) if t.estimate is not none else "—" }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4g"|format(t.p_value) if t.p_value is not none else "—" }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4g"|format(t.q_value) if t.q_value is not none else "—" }}</td>
        {% endfor %}
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Harmonized vs unharmonized — sensitivity analysis</h3>
{% if charts.get("sensitivity") %}
  <figure class="chart">{{ charts["sensitivity"] | safe }}</figure>
{% endif %}
  {% if not sensitivity %}
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Not run.</strong> The model was fitted on one arm only, so there is
    no evidence here about how far the conclusions depend on the harmonization
    choice. Absence of this comparison is not evidence that the choice does not
    matter.
  </div>
  {% elif not sensitivity.applicable %}
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Not applicable.</strong> Harmonization changed no values in this
    run, so the two arms are the same fit reported twice.
  </div>
  {% else %}
  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Neither arm is inference.</strong> The harmonized arm is the
    primary run and the unharmonized arm shows how much the conclusions move
    when that assumption is dropped. Where scanner is confounded with time, a
    scanner step and a biological change of the same size are the same
    observation, so agreement between the arms is not evidence of correctness
    and disagreement identifies which conclusions were never supportable.
  </div>

  <dl class="kv">
    <dt>regions estimated under both arms</dt>
    <dd>{{ sensitivity.n_comparable }} of {{ sensitivity.rows | length }}</dd>
    <dt>direction changes</dt><dd>{{ sensitivity.n_sign_flips }}</dd>
    <dt>significance changes</dt><dd>{{ sensitivity.n_significance_changes }}</dd>
  </dl>

  <div class="scroll">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Region</th>
        <th class="num">Harmonized</th><th class="num">Unharmonized</th>
        <th class="num">Difference</th>
        <th class="num">q (harm.)</th><th class="num">q (unharm.)</th>
        <th>Direction</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in sensitivity.rows %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.region }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4f"|format(row.harmonized) if row.harmonized is not none else "—" }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4f"|format(row.unharmonized) if row.unharmonized is not none else "—" }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4f"|format(row.difference) if row.difference is not none else "—" }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4g"|format(row.harmonized_q) if row.harmonized_q is not none else "—" }}</td>
        <td class="num">{{ "%.4g"|format(row.unharmonized_q) if row.unharmonized_q is not none else "—" }}</td>
        <td>
          {% if not row.comparable %}<span class="badge">n/a</span>
          {% elif row.sign_flipped %}<span class="badge bad">flips</span>
          {% else %}<span class="badge ok">agrees</span>{% endif %}
        </td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {% if sensitivity and sensitivity.notes %}
  <div class="callout">
    <ul class="tight">
      {% for note in sensitivity.notes %}<li>{{ note }}</li>{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {% if model.notes %}
  <div class="callout">
    <ul class="tight">
      {% for note in model.notes %}<li>{{ note }}</li>{% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {# ---- 6. Limitations ----
     Standing limitations are presentation text and live here rather than in
     report.py, so that naming input-format specifics does not drag format
     vocabulary into a downstream stage module (§1.4). Run-dependent findings
     are supplied by the stage. ---- #}
  <h2>6. Limitations</h2>

  {% for item in dynamic_limitations %}
  <div class="lim">
    <strong>{{ item.title }}</strong>
    <p>{{ item.body }}</p>
  </div>
  {% endfor %}

  <div class="lim">
    <strong>Secondary families are exploratory, not hypotheses</strong>
    <p>
      Only the <code>time &times; diagnosis</code> interaction is a declared
      hypothesis. The main effects of <code>time</code>,
      <code>dx_baseline</code>, and <code>age_baseline</code> are each
      corrected within their own family and reported for completeness; they
      were not pre-specified, and reading a small <em>q</em> among them as a
      finding is exactly the multiplicity error the family declaration exists
      to prevent.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="lim">
    <strong>pybids is not a derivatives parser</strong>
    <p>
      pybids indexes raw BIDS well, but its BIDS-Derivatives support is
      partial, and FreeSurfer's native subject-directory output is not
      BIDS-organized at all. morphline uses a purpose-built resolver for
      FreeSurfer conventions and reserves pybids for raw layout traversal
      inside adapters.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="lim">
    <strong>Harmonized results are sensitivity analysis, not inference</strong>
    <p>
      Running harmonized and unharmonized models shows how conclusions depend
      on the harmonization assumption. It does not establish which is correct.
      Where scanner is confounded with time, the biological longitudinal
      effect and the scanner effect are not identifiable from the observed
      data alone — a property of the study design, not a deficiency in the
      software.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="lim">
    <strong>Missingness is assumed missing-at-random</strong>
    <p>
      Mixed-effects models use all available observations and handle
      unbalanced longitudinal designs naturally, but validity rests on a MAR
      assumption. That assumption is questionable in aging cohorts, where
      dropout can be caused by the disease progression under study, and where
      QC failure is plausibly associated with atrophy severity and motion.
      v1 does not attempt MNAR modeling — no pattern-mixture models, selection
      models, or joint dropout modeling.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="lim">
    <strong>Synthetic data only</strong>
    <p>
      These results come from synthetic fixtures with injected ground truth.
      That validates <em>statistical recovery</em> — whether the methods
      recover known truth — and cannot validate that the parser handles real
      headers and real metadata. Those are different questions, and both are
      needed. Real-data integration is a later milestone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <footer>
    Generated by morphline {{ provenance.pipeline_version }}. This report is
    self-contained: it fetches no external assets and can be archived or read
    offline.
  </footer>

</div>
{{ theme_script | safe }}
</body>
</html>
